      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Hereditary angioedema ordinarily manifests itself in childhood. The development of angioedema in this disease and as a side effect of angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor treatment is caused by similar mechanisms."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "We report a case of female patient, diagnosed with hereditary angioedema at age 90, who experienced the first attack 8 years earlier during angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor use."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "This patient presented with her first episode of hereditary angioedema at the oldest age ever reported. A careful family history, especially for angioedema, should be taken in patients of all ages before initiating treatment with angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor."
        }
      ]},
